Example programs and the texts they cite (examples/)

- examples/migration/*/sources/ — other systems' programs, copied so that
  their translations into Logical English can be checked against them. Each
  keeps its authors' copyright and licence, stated in a LICENSE or NOTICE
  file in its folder or in the folder's MAKING_OF.md:
  - blawx/: example projects of Blawx, by Lexpedite Legal Technologies; MIT
    licence (BLAWX_LICENSE beside each project).
  - l4/: L4 examples, copyright Singapore Management University; Apache
    License 2.0.
  - legalruleml/: examples of the LegalRuleML Core Specification Version 1.0,
    copyright OASIS Open, under the OASIS intellectual property rights policy.
  - miniscript/: spending policies from Bitcoin Core (MIT licence,
    COPYING), Liana (copyright the Liana developers, 3-clause BSD licence,
    LICENCE) and Pieter Wuille's Miniscript page; the pages and files they
    come from are kept unchanged in sources/originals/.
  - scasp/: programs by the authors of Logical English 1 and of s(CASP),
    copied unchanged.
  The OIPA examples (oipa/) are not Oracle's: they were written for this
  repository, following the forms of Oracle's public OIPA XML Configuration
  Guide.
- examples/regulatory/ — models of public legal texts, which quote those
  texts for citation: United States federal texts (the Harmonized Tariff
  Schedule, Customs rulings, Medicare coverage documents, regulations and
  decisions), which are in the public domain; European Binding Tariff
  Information decisions, published by the European Commission for reuse; and
  published insurance filings and historical documents (underwriting/).
- examples/regulatory/bna.le — the British Nationality Act 1981 as a logic
  program, recovered from a 1980s micro-PROLOG program by M. J. Sergot,
  F. Sadri, R. A. Kowalski, F. Kriwaczek, P. Hammond and H. T. Cory ("The
  British Nationality Act as a logic program", Communications of the ACM,
  1986).
- examples/moreExamples/collections/kowalski-book/ — programs based on the
  examples of Robert Kowalski's book "Computational Logic and Human
  Thinking" (Cambridge University Press, 2011).
